---
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { DOCS, SITE } from "~/content/site";
---
<Base>
  <section class="rdx-hero rdx-shell" id="brief">
    <div class="rdx-hero-copy">
      <div class="rdx-eyebrow">SELF-HOSTED // LOCAL-FIRST // APACHE-2.0</div>
      <h1>Redact<br />before you<br /><span class="rdx-redact-word">prompt</span>.</h1>
      <p class="rdx-lead">Redax is a local-first PII redaction boundary. Sensitive identifiers are detected and transformed before your application calls an LLM — while you keep control of the host, model files, network, and retention.</p>
      <div class="rdx-actions"><a class="rdx-button rdx-button-primary" href="#demo">Open the live demo <span>↘</span></a><a class="rdx-button" href={DOCS.api}>Read the docs <span>↗</span></a></div>
      <div class="rdx-microfacts"><span>NO HOSTED INFERENCE</span><span>CPU-FRIENDLY PATH</span><span>CONTROL EGRESS</span></div>
    </div>
    <aside class="rdx-file" aria-label="Illustrative redaction case file">
      <div class="rdx-file-tab">CASE FILE // RDX-001</div>
      <div class="rdx-file-head"><h2>Transmission intercept<br />Pre-model boundary</h2><div>DATE 2026.09.30<br />STATUS SAFE TO FORWARD</div></div>
      <p class="rdx-doc-copy">Subject identifies as <span class="rdx-bar">Jordan Reyes</span> from <span class="rdx-bar">Stripe</span>.<br />Contact channel: <span class="rdx-bar">jordan@stripe.com</span>.<br />Voice endpoint: <span class="rdx-bar">+1 (415) 555-2671</span>.</p>
      <pre class="rdx-json">&#123;
  "text": "Hi, I'm [PERSON_0000] from [ORG_0000].
  Reach me at [EMAIL_0000] or [PHONE_E164_0000].",
  "spans": 4,
  "boundary": "local"
&#125;</pre>
      <div class="rdx-classified">SANITIZED</div>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="rdx-section" id="product"><div class="rdx-shell rdx-section-grid"><div class="rdx-section-label">FILE 01 / PRODUCT DOCTRINE</div><div><h2>Privacy is the architecture.<br /><span class="rdx-redact-word">Not a feature flag.</span></h2><p class="rdx-sub">Treat the redaction layer like a controlled checkpoint. Raw values enter once, transformed text leaves, and operational metadata remains inspectable.</p><div class="rdx-cards">
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">01 / DETERMINISM</div><h3>Predictable replacements</h3><p>Structured rules and typed placeholders make the transformation easy to inspect and test.</p></article>
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">02 / BOUNDARY</div><h3>Local by default</h3><p>Run the service beside your application and keep inference and egress under your control.</p></article>
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">03 / INTEGRATION</div><h3>One checkpoint</h3><p>Put the redaction call immediately before your existing model request instead of replacing the whole stack.</p></article>
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">04 / AUDIT</div><h3>Evidence, not exposure</h3><p>Record counts, types, timing, and request metadata without writing original sensitive values into the audit event.</p></article>
  </div></div></div></section>

  <section class="rdx-section rdx-explain" id="how-it-works"><div class="rdx-shell rdx-section-grid"><div class="rdx-section-label">FILE 01A / THE SHORT VERSION</div><div><h2>What it is, why it exists, and where it belongs.</h2><div class="rdx-explain-grid">
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">WHAT</div><h3>A boundary before the model</h3><p>Redax accepts text from your application, detects configured sensitive entities, applies a policy, and returns transformed text. Your existing model provider, prompt system, and application remain yours.</p></article>
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">WHY</div><h3>Reduce accidental exposure</h3><p>LLM requests often carry support tickets, logs, documents, or conversations containing identifiers. Redax gives that text one inspectable checkpoint before it crosses into a model or provider.</p></article>
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">HOW</div><h3>One HTTP call</h3><p>Deploy the API beside your application, call <code>POST /v1/redact</code>, forward only the returned text, and keep the response metadata for your own operational checks.</p></article>
  </div><div class="rdx-callout"><strong>Important boundary:</strong> Redax is not a guarantee that every secret or PII value will be detected. Review policies, model coverage, provider logs, infrastructure access, and retention for your own risk profile.</div></div></div></section>

  <section class="rdx-section" id="deploy"><div class="rdx-shell rdx-section-grid"><div class="rdx-section-label">FILE 01B / DEPLOYMENT PATH</div><div><h2>Can a developer use it?<br />Yes — start local, then promote the same contract.</h2><p class="rdx-sub">The fastest path is Docker Compose. It gives a developer the API, worker, Redis, metrics, logs, and traces on one machine without AWS or another managed service. When the integration is proven, move the same API and worker into your own platform.</p><div class="rdx-deploy-grid">
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">01 / LOCAL</div><h3>Docker Compose</h3><p>Run <code>docker compose up --build</code>, call the local API, and use the bundled observability stack. Good for integration, policy review, and repeatable smoke tests.</p><a class="rdx-inline-link" href="/redax/docs/deployment">Local deployment guide ↗</a></article>
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">02 / STANDARD</div><h3>Private service</h3><p>Run one or a few API processes and a separate worker. Put the service behind your private network, configure production secrets, and use durable Redis for jobs, idempotency, cache, and rate limits.</p><a class="rdx-inline-link" href="/redax/docs/architecture">Read the architecture ↗</a></article>
    <article><div class="rdx-card-num">03 / SCALED</div><h3>Reference Kubernetes</h3><p>Use the checked-in topology for replicas, probes, rollout budgets, resource limits, autoscaling, and network policies. Supply your own durable Redis, ingress, identity, and telemetry boundaries.</p><a class="rdx-inline-link" href="/redax/docs/launch">Open the launch gate ↗</a></article>
  </div><div class="rdx-config-grid"><div class="rdx-config-heading">Configured during deployment</div><ul><li><b>Hosting / region</b><span>Choose where the API, worker, model, and audit data run.</span></li><li><b>Redis HA / backups</b><span>Set the durable Redis URL, namespace, backup policy, failover owner, and RPO/RTO.</span></li><li><b>Telemetry</b><span>Point OTLP, metrics, and logs at an approved destination with retention and privacy rules.</span></li><li><b>Secrets / identity</b><span>Inject API keys, hash salt, job encryption, audit integrity, and rotation/revocation controls.</span></li><li><b>DNS / TLS</b><span>Terminate TLS at the ingress or proxy, set trusted hosts, and expose only the required routes.</span></li><li><b>SLOs</b><span>Set latency, availability, queue age, recovery, and error-budget targets from measured workload evidence.</span></li></ul></div><p class="rdx-sub rdx-note"><strong>What this means:</strong> these are deployment inputs, not hidden product defaults. Redax supplies the application contract and reference checks; the operator supplies the environment, ownership, retention, and acceptance thresholds.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="rdx-section" id="demo"><div class="rdx-shell rdx-section-grid"><div class="rdx-section-label">FILE 02 / LIVE DEMONSTRATION</div><div><h2>Type a sentence.<br />Watch the PII disappear.</h2><p class="rdx-sub">Start with the browser-local path: it applies the same deterministic structured rules without sending your text to a backend. The server-side model and the experimental browser ONNX bundle are separate deployment choices.</p><div class="rdx-demo-mode"><span>EXECUTION MODE</span><button class="active" type="button">BROWSER / REGEX · READY</button><a href="/redax/docs/deployment">SERVER / GLiNER2 · DEPLOYMENT GUIDE ↗</a></div><div class="rdx-demo"><div class="rdx-panel"><div class="rdx-panel-head"><span>RAW INPUT</span><span id="rdx-count">0 CHARS</span></div><textarea id="rdx-raw" spellcheck="false" maxlength="100000">Email me at alice@example.com or call +1-415-555-2671. My card is 4111 1111 1111 1111.</textarea><div class="rdx-status"><span>BOUNDARY: LOCAL BROWSER</span><span>INPUT // UNCLASSIFIED</span></div></div><div class="rdx-panel rdx-panel-dark"><div class="rdx-panel-head"><span>SAFE PREVIEW</span><span id="rdx-span-count">0 SPANS</span></div><div id="rdx-safe"></div><div class="rdx-status"><span id="rdx-mode-status">REGEX · NO NETWORK</span><span>OUTPUT // SANITIZED</span></div></div></div><div class="rdx-demo-note"><b>Browser execution:</b> this demo is client-side and illustrative. It detects structured email, phone, URL, IP, IBAN, SSN, and Luhn-valid card values. The public page does not load the larger GLiNER2 model; the repository’s ONNX/WASM path requires a separately bundled model and is documented as experimental.</div></div></div></section>

  <section class="rdx-section" id="pipeline"><div class="rdx-shell rdx-section-grid"><div class="rdx-section-label">FILE 03 / REQUEST LIFECYCLE</div><div><h2>One request.<br />Five controlled stages.</h2><div class="rdx-pipeline">
    <div><b>01</b><section><h3>Regex safety net</h3><p>Catch structured identifiers such as email, phone, IP, card, URL, SSN and IBAN.</p></section><span>stage_01 → detect</span></div>
    <div><b>02</b><section><h3>Contextual entity model</h3><p>Optionally identify people, organisations, and contextual entities using the local model path.</p></section><span>stage_02 → classify</span></div>
    <div><b>03</b><section><h3>Consensus fusion</h3><p>Merge detector spans, resolve overlap, and preserve the strongest label.</p></section><span>stage_03 → resolve</span></div>
    <div><b>04</b><section><h3>Replacement</h3><p>Transform sensitive values into deterministic typed placeholders before the downstream model sees them.</p></section><span>stage_04 → redact</span></div>
    <div><b>05</b><section><h3>Audit boundary</h3><p>Retain operational evidence while keeping original values out of the audit event.</p></section><span>stage_05 ● logged</span></div>
  </div></div></div></section>

  <section class="rdx-section rdx-start" id="start"><div class="rdx-shell rdx-start-inner"><div class="rdx-eyebrow">END OF FILE // CONTINUE LOCALLY</div><h2>Put a safe boundary<br />in front of your model.</h2><p>Run Redax with Docker Compose, send one request, and inspect the transformed output before connecting your existing LLM workflow.</p><div class="rdx-actions"><a class="rdx-button rdx-button-primary" href={DOCS.deployment}>Deployment guide <span>↗</span></a><a class="rdx-button" href={SITE.repo}>View the source <span>↗</span></a></div></div></section>
</Base>

<script>
  import { redact } from "~/scripts/redact";
  const raw = document.getElementById("rdx-raw") as HTMLTextAreaElement | null;
  const safe = document.getElementById("rdx-safe");
  const count = document.getElementById("rdx-count");
  const spanCount = document.getElementById("rdx-span-count");
  function render() {
    if (!raw || !safe) return;
    const started = performance.now();
    const result = redact(raw.value);
    if (count) count.textContent = `${raw.value.length} CHARS`;
    if (spanCount) spanCount.textContent = `${result.spans.length} SPAN${result.spans.length === 1 ? "" : "S"} · ${(performance.now() - started).toFixed(1)} MS`;
    safe.textContent = result.text || "No input.";
  }
  raw?.addEventListener("input", render); render();
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".rdx-bar").forEach((bar) => bar.addEventListener("click", () => bar.classList.toggle("is-revealed")));
</script>
